{0 How displays work}

The "execution engine" runs in a web worker, not the main thread of the
page. This means that in order have interactive elements in the page,
the two need to communicate, and this is done by sending messages.
It's important to understand what messages get sent back and forth, and
when, in order to get a robust mental model of how displays work.

This is how the parts fit together.

{1 The parts}

The page runs [x-ocaml.js] in its main thread, and the cells run in a web
worker, [x-ocaml.worker.js]. The two talk only through the messages in
{{!section-messages}the table at the end}.

{t
  | Part | Thread | Source | What it does |
  |------|--------|--------|--------------|
  | Cells | page | [src/x_ocaml.ml], [src/cell.ml], [src/client.ml] | the [<x-ocaml>] editors; Run sends a cell's code to the worker |
  | Displays | page | [src/displays.ml] | a display area after each cell that has any; shows, updates and removes elements; adds a required script to the page; sends an [x-ocaml-callback] event on as a {!X_protocol.constructor-Callback} |
  | Elements | page | a library's script, or [src/displays.ml] | a library's custom element, or [<x-ocaml-html>], the one x-ocaml defines |
  | Worker loop | worker | [worker/x_worker.ml] | runs cells, and callbacks, one at a time, as the page asks |
  | Toplevel | worker | [worker/eval.ml] | runs a cell's phrases |
  | {!X_ocaml_lib} | worker | [worker-lib/] | what a cell's code calls to show displays and register callbacks |
  | {!X_ocaml_cells} | worker | [worker-lib/cells/] | the worker's side: which cell each display and callback belongs to |
  | The cell's code | worker | the page's cells | calls {!X_ocaml_lib}, directly or through a library, and makes each display's data |
}

A library that puts things on the page comes in two halves: OCaml, in the
worker, that a cell's code calls and that calls {!X_ocaml_lib}; and a script
that defines its element, which {!X_ocaml_lib.require} has the page load.

{!X_ocaml_lib} is what code in a cell calls. What the worker itself keeps
and calls, the phrase being run and which cell each display and callback
belongs to, is {!X_ocaml_cells}, which cells cannot see.

A {b display} is the worker's handle on one element on the page:
{!X_ocaml_lib.val-display} or {!X_ocaml_lib.html} shows the element and
returns the handle, which {!X_ocaml_lib.update} and {!X_ocaml_lib.remove}
take. The handle is abstract, a {!X_ocaml_lib.type-display}, and a cell can
show as many displays as it likes. Between the worker and the page, a display
is named by a number instead, counted across the page. So in the charts below
the messages carry numbers, such as display 7, and the cell's code holds a
handle, [d].

A cell's {b display area} is a [<div class="x-ocaml-displays">] that the
page puts straight after the cell's [<x-ocaml>] element when the cell first
shows a display, and takes away when its last display goes. The cell's
displays go in it, in the order they were shown, including those a callback
shows later. It has the one listener for [x-ocaml-callback]: an element's
event bubbles up to it, and it posts the {!X_protocol.constructor-Callback},
so an element never needs to know which cell it belongs to.

An element is any custom element with a [data] setter. It is given its data,
a string that only it and its library read, JSON by convention. It calls back
by dispatching a bubbling [x-ocaml-callback] event whose [detail] is
[{ token, payload }].

In the charts below, [===>] is a message between the threads, [--->] a call
or an event within one, and [*] something done where it is.

{1 A cell shows an element}

{v
                         main thread  :  web worker
           element    x-ocaml page         worker loop   X_ocaml_lib    cell code
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              |             | Eval (2, code)    |             |             |
 1            |             |==================>|             |             |
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              |             |         :         | forget_cell 2             |
 2            |             |         :         |------------>|             |
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              |             |         :         | run the phrases           |
 3            |             |         :         |-------------------------->|
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              |             |         :         |             | require     |
 4            |             |         :         |             |<------------|
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              |             | Require (tag, src)|             |             |
 5            |             |<================================|             |
              |             |         :         |             |             |
 6            |             | * add <script src>|             |             |
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              |             |         :         |             | display     |
 7            |             |         :         |             |<------------|
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              |             | Display (2, 7, tag, data)       |             |
 8            |             |<================================|             |
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              | <tag> in area         :         |             |             |
 9            |<------------|         :         |             |             |
              |             |         :         |             |             |
10            |             | * data waits for definition     |             |
              |             |         :         |             |             |
11            | * defined: el.data = data       |             |             |
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              |             | Top_response (2, ..)            |             |
12            |             |<==================|             |             |
              |             |         :         |             |             |
v}

+ The reader runs cell 2, or the page loads.
+ Before every [Eval], {!X_ocaml_cells.forget_cell} forgets what the cell's
  last run showed. The first time, there is nothing to forget.
+ The toplevel runs the cell's phrases. The worker notes which cell is running
  ({!X_ocaml_cells}), so the cell's code never needs to say which cell it
  is: every display it makes, and every callback it registers, is recorded
  as cell 2's.
+ The cell uses a library, a chart library say, whose displays need an element
  of its own, [<x-chart>]. The library asks for it with
  {!X_ocaml_lib.require}, giving the tag and the script that defines it. The
  first time a tag is required, the worker sends the page a
  {!X_protocol.constructor-Require} message; later calls for the same tag
  send nothing.
+ The {!X_protocol.constructor-Require} message is posted at once, while the
  cell is still running.
+ The page adds a [<script>] for [src], resolved against the worker's URL,
  unless the element is already defined or the script already added.
+ The cell's code, or its library, calls {!X_ocaml_lib.val-display} with the
  tag and the data. It records the new display as cell 2's, with its
  [on_remove], asks the page to show an element with that tag, and returns
  the display's handle.
+ The request is a {!X_protocol.constructor-Display} message: the cell, the
  display's number (7 here), the tag and the data.
+ The element goes in cell 2's display area, made now for its first display.
+ The script may not have run yet. A property set before an element is
  defined would hide its setter, so the page keeps the latest data and gives
  it on [customElements.whenDefined].
+ The script defines the element; the page sets its [data], and it draws.
+ The cell has finished. Its display stays until the cell runs again.

{1 Updates while the cell runs}

{v
                         main thread  :  web worker
           element    x-ocaml page         worker loop   X_ocaml_lib    cell code
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              |             | Eval (3, code)    |             |             |
 1            |             |==================>|             |             |
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              |             |         :         |             | html        |
 2            |             |         :         |             |<------------|
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              |             | Display (3, 4, "x-ocaml-html")  |             |
 3            |             |<================================|             |
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              |             |         :         |             | update d    |
 4            |             |         :         |             |<------------|
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              |             | Update (4, "<b>10%</b>")        |             |
 5            |             |<================================|             |
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              | el.data     |         :         |             |             |
 6            |<------------|         :         |             |             |
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              |             |         :         |             | update d    |
 7            |             |         :         |             |<------------|
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              |             | Update (4, "<b>20%</b>")        |             |
 8            |             |<================================|             |
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              | el.data     |         :         |             |             |
 9            |<------------|         :         |             |             |
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              |             | Top_response (3, ..)            |             |
10            |             |<==================|             |             |
              |             |         :         |             |             |
v}

- {b 2–3.} {!X_ocaml_lib.html} is {!X_ocaml_lib.val-display} with the tag
  [x-ocaml-html].
- {b 4–9.} Each {!X_ocaml_lib.update} is posted as it is made, while the
  worker is still busy with the cell. The page's thread is free, so each
  shows as it arrives.

{1 The reader uses an element}

{v
                         main thread  :  web worker
reader     element    x-ocaml page         worker loop   X_ocaml_lib    cell code
   |          |             |         :         |             |             |
   |          |             |         :         |             | callback f  |
 1 |          |             |         :         |             |<------------|
   |          |             |         :         |             |             |
   |          |             |         :         |             | display     |
 2 |          |             |         :         |             |<------------|
   |          |             |         :         |             |             |
   |          |             | Display (2, 7, tag, {..12..})   |             |
 3 |          |             |<================================|             |
   |          |             |         :         |             |             |
   |          | el.data     |         :         |             |             |
 4 |          |<------------|         :         |             |             |
   |          |             |         :         |             |             |
   | click    |             |         :         |             |             |
 5 |--------->|             |         :         |             |             |
   |          |             |         :         |             |             |
   |          | x-ocaml-callback      :         |             |             |
 6 |          |------------>|         :         |             |             |
   |          |             |         :         |             |             |
   |          |             | Callback (12, payload)          |             |
 7 |          |             |==================>|             |             |
   |          |             |         :         |             |             |
   |          |             |         :         | invoke      |             |
 8 |          |             |         :         |------------>|             |
   |          |             |         :         |             |             |
   |          |             |         :         |             | f payload   |
 9 |          |             |         :         |             |------------>|
   |          |             |         :         |             |             |
   |          |             |         :         |             | update d    |
10 |          |             |         :         |             |<------------|
   |          |             |         :         |             |             |
   |          |             | Update (7, data)  |             |             |
11 |          |             |<================================|             |
   |          |             |         :         |             |             |
   |          | el.data     |         :         |             |             |
12 |          |<------------|         :         |             |             |
   |          |             |         :         |             |             |
v}

+ The cell registers a function: {!X_ocaml_lib.callback} records it as cell
  2's, and returns its token, an abstract {!X_ocaml_lib.type-token}. The
  page will know it as 12.
+ The token goes in the display's data, as {!X_ocaml_lib.string_of_token}
  gives it, wherever the library likes: here, [{"click": 12, ...}]. x-ocaml
  does not know it is there.
+ It reaches the page inside the data,
+ and the element keeps it.
+ Later, the reader clicks.
+ The element dispatches [x-ocaml-callback], with [{ token: 12, payload }].
  The payload is whatever the element likes: where the click was, say.
+ The event bubbles up to cell 2's display area, which posts it as a
  {!X_protocol.constructor-Callback}. If a cell is running, it waits its
  turn.
+ {!X_ocaml_cells.invoke} looks up the token. An exception from the function
  is caught and logged; a token nobody knows does nothing.
+ [f payload] runs as part of cell 2, so anything it shows goes under cell 2.
+ Here it updates the display, and the element redraws.

{1 Controls in an HTML display}

{v
                         main thread  :  web worker
reader     element    x-ocaml page         worker loop   X_ocaml_lib    cell code
   |          |             |         :         |             |             |
   |          |             |         :         |             | callback say|
 1 |          |             |         :         |             |<------------|
   |          |             |         :         |             |             |
   |          |             |         :         |             | html        |
 2 |          |             |         :         |             |<------------|
   |          |             |         :         |             |             |
   |          |             | Display (3, 8, "x-ocaml-html")  |             |
 3 |          |             |<================================|             |
   |          |             |         :         |             |             |
   |          | innerHTML   |         :         |             |             |
 4 |          |<------------|         :         |             |             |
   |          |             |         :         |             |             |
   | input    |             |         :         |             |             |
 5 |--------->|             |         :         |             |             |
   |          |             |         :         |             |             |
 6 |          | * closest [data-callback]       |             |             |
   |          |             |         :         |             |             |
   |          | x-ocaml-callback      :         |             |             |
 7 |          |------------>|         :         |             |             |
   |          |             |         :         |             |             |
   |          |             | Callback (5, "70")|             |             |
 8 |          |             |==================>|             |             |
   |          |             |         :         |             |             |
   |          |             |         :         | invoke      |             |
 9 |          |             |         :         |------------>|             |
   |          |             |         :         |             |             |
   |          |             |         :         |             | say "70"    |
10 |          |             |         :         |             |------------>|
   |          |             |         :         |             |             |
v}

- {b 1–2.} The token goes in a [data-callback] attribute, as
  {!X_ocaml_lib.string_of_token} gives it: [html {|<input type="range"
  data-callback="5">|}].
- {b 5–7.} [<x-ocaml-html>] listens for clicks and input. The nearest
  element with [data-callback] gives the token; the payload is an input's
  value (["true"] or ["false"] for a checkbox), or else the element's
  [data-payload]. From there on it is as for any other element.

An update replaces the whole HTML, and with it a control the reader is using,
so controls go in a display of their own that is not updated.

{1 The cell runs again}

{v
                         main thread  :  web worker
           element    x-ocaml page         worker loop   X_ocaml_lib    cell code
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              |             | Eval (2, code)    |             |             |
 1            |             |==================>|             |             |
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              |             |         :         | forget_cell 2             |
 2            |             |         :         |------------>|             |
              |             |         :         |             |             |
 3            |             |         :   forget its tokens * |             |
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              |             | Remove 7          |             |             |
 4            |             |<================================|             |
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              |             |         :         |             | on_remove ()|
 5            |             |         :         |             |------------>|
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              | remove      |         :         |             |             |
 6            |<------------|         :         |             |             |
              |             |         :         |             |             |
              |             |         :         | run the phrases           |
 7            |             |         :         |-------------------------->|
              |             |         :         |             |             |
v}

- {b 2.} Before the new code runs, {!X_ocaml_cells.forget_cell} forgets the
  cell's last run:
- {b 3.} its tokens, so a {!X_protocol.constructor-Callback} with one does
  nothing, even from an element that has not gone yet;
- {b 4–6.} and its displays: for each, a {!X_protocol.constructor-Remove},
  and its [on_remove], so code drawing it, an animation say, knows to stop.
  The page takes the element away, and the display area when it is empty.
- {b 7.} Then the new code runs, and shows what it shows.

{1 Writing an element}

This is about an element's definition: the script that registers a tag with
[customElements.define]. Each display with that tag is one instance of it.

A library's page half is a script that defines a custom element. Here is a
whole one, [counter.js]: a button that shows how often it has been clicked,
and calls back when it is.

{@js[
// <x-counter>: a button that shows how often it was clicked, and calls back.
// data: {"count": number, "click": token}
customElements.define('x-counter', class extends HTMLElement {
  connectedCallback() {
    this.button = document.createElement('button');
    this.button.onclick = () =>
      this.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('x-ocaml-callback', {
        bubbles: true,
        detail: { token: this.d.click, payload: '' },
      }));
    this.append(this.button);
  }
  set data(s) {
    this.d = JSON.parse(s);
    this.button.textContent = `clicked ${this.d.count} times`;
  }
});
]}

And the OCaml that loads it, shows one, and counts the clicks:

{[
let () = X_ocaml_lib.require ~tag:"x-counter" ~src:"counter.js"

let count = ref 0
let redraw = ref (fun () -> ())

let click =
  X_ocaml_lib.callback (fun _ ->
      incr count;
      !redraw ())

let data () =
  Printf.sprintf {|{"count":%d,"click":%s}|} !count
    (X_ocaml_lib.string_of_token click)
let counter = X_ocaml_lib.display ~tag:"x-counter" (data ())
let () = redraw := fun () -> X_ocaml_lib.update counter (data ())
]}

What x-ocaml does with such an element, and what it expects of it:

- {b The script} is added to the page as a classic [<script>], not a module,
  from [src] resolved against the worker's URL. It is added once for each
  tag, and not at all if the tag is already defined. It should define the
  element with that tag: x-ocaml waits for [customElements.whenDefined].
- {b The tag} must be a valid custom element name, with a hyphen in it.
  [x-ocaml-html] is x-ocaml's own. A display whose tag is not a custom
  element name, such as [div], is not shown, and the page warns.
- {b [require] is optional}, and can come before or after
  {!X_ocaml_lib.val-display}. The element is made at once, and given its
  data when its definition arrives, however that happens: [require], the
  page's own script, or another cell's. An element whose definition never
  arrives stays empty, with no warning.
- {b The element} is made in the cell's display area, in the page's own DOM,
  so the page's CSS applies to it.
- {b [data]} is set only once the element is connected and defined, so
  [connectedCallback] has run by then. It is set again at each update: the
  setter must cope with being called many times. If updates come before the
  element is defined, it is given only the latest.
- {b Callbacks} are [x-ocaml-callback] events that bubble ([bubbles: true]) up
  to the display area. If the element keeps its insides in a
  {{:https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_components/Using_shadow_DOM}shadow
  root} and dispatches the event from inside it, from a button there say,
  the event must also be
  {{:https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Event/composed}[composed:
  true]}: otherwise it stops at the shadow root and never reaches the
  display area. An event dispatched on the element itself, as the counter's
  is, needs only [bubbles: true]. The [detail] is [{ token, payload }]: the
  token must be an integer, or nothing is called; a payload that is not a
  string is sent as its JSON, and a missing one as [""].
- {b A callback gets no reply.} The display area posts it to the worker, and
  nothing comes back: the element cannot tell whether its token was known,
  whether the function raised (the worker says so in the console), or whether
  it is still waiting behind a cell that is running. Callbacks run one at a
  time, in the order they were sent, and none is merged or dropped: a slider
  that calls back on every [input] event, into a slow function, falls behind
  the reader. An element that must keep up can treat the update that answers
  a callback as its acknowledgement, and call back again only once it has
  come.
- {b The token} is whatever the library's OCaml put in the data; x-ocaml does
  not look inside the data, and does not know which element has which token.
- {b Removal}: when the cell runs again, or its code calls
  {!X_ocaml_lib.remove}, the element is taken off the page.
  [disconnectedCallback] is where to stop anything it has running.

{1:messages The messages}

All in {!X_protocol}.

{t
  | Message | Direction | Sent by | Handled by |
  |---------|-----------|---------|------------|
  | {!X_protocol.constructor-Display} | worker to page | {!X_ocaml_lib.val-display}, {!X_ocaml_lib.html} | [Displays.show], on the page |
  | {!X_protocol.constructor-Update} | worker to page | {!X_ocaml_lib.update} | [Displays.update] |
  | {!X_protocol.constructor-Remove} | worker to page | {!X_ocaml_lib.remove}, {!X_ocaml_cells.forget_cell} | [Displays.remove] |
  | {!X_protocol.constructor-Require} | worker to page | {!X_ocaml_lib.require}, once per tag | [Displays.require] |
  | {!X_protocol.constructor-Callback} | page to worker | the display area's listener | {!X_ocaml_cells.invoke}, in the worker |
}
